<div class="help-article">
    <p class="help-lead">
        A few claims don't simply follow the calendar year or the full amount paid. The <a href="tax">Tax report</a> has tools
        for the common ones. As everywhere in LoonieBooks, these are record-keeping aids, not tax advice.
    </p>

    <h3>Medical expenses: the 12-month window</h3>
    <p>
        Medical expenses can be claimed for any 12-month period ending in the tax year, and only the part above the lesser of 3%
        of net income or the CRA's annual amount counts. On the Medical expenses card, open
        <span class="help-ui">12-month window and threshold</span>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Optionally enter your <strong>net income</strong> (line 23600) so it can estimate the claimable part.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Find the best window</span> checks all twelve windows ending in the year, from your confirmed medical
            expenses, and shows the one with the most.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Claim this window</span> moves the report to that window; <span class="help-ui">Use the calendar year</span> goes back.
            A window reaching into the previous year takes those expenses out of the previous year's report, so only use it if
            you didn't claim them then.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Business-use share</h3>
    <p>
        For employment, self-employment and rental expenses, sometimes only part of a bill is for earning income: the home-office
        share of rent and utilities, the work share of a phone, internet or car, or the interest part of a mortgage payment on a
        rental. Set the percentage and only that part is counted.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>On one transaction: the <strong>%</strong> box under its amount on the report.</li>
        <li>On many: the <span class="help-ui">Business-use share</span> box on the card. Enter a percent, choose every item or one spending
            category (say, Bills &amp; Utilities), and <span class="help-ui">Apply</span>.</li>
        <li>Keep a record of how you worked it out, such as floor area or a vehicle logbook.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>RRSP contributions in the first 60 days</h3>
    <p>
        Contributions made from January 1 to the RRSP deadline (about March 1) can be deducted for the previous year. LoonieBooks
        counts them in the year they were paid, and lists them on the previous year's report under
        <span class="help-ui">RRSP contributions in the first 60 days of …</span>. <span class="help-ui">Deduct for …</span> moves one there.
        Your RRSP issuer's receipt shows which period each contribution belongs to.
    </p>

    <h3>Claiming in another year</h3>
    <p>
        A claim moved to a different year shows on that year's report with <em>Paid in …, claimed for …</em>, and on the year it was
        paid under <span class="help-ui">Paid in …, claimed in another year</span>, where it isn't counted. Either side has a link to
        claim it in the other year instead.
    </p>
</div>
